I kept the usual selection sort idea here. For each position, the code searches the unsorted part for the smallest value and swaps it into place. After each round, the left side is finished.

For example, with `[4, 2, 5, 1]`, the first round places `1` at index `0`, then the next round places `2` at index `1`. The input vector is changed directly.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> selectionSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(nums.size());

        for (int start = 0; start < n - 1; ++start) {
            int smallest = start;

            for (int current = start + 1; current < n; ++current) {
                if (nums[current] < nums[smallest]) {
                    smallest = current;
                }
            }

            swap(nums[start], nums[smallest]);
        }

        return nums;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because every position scans the remaining values.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

This version uses recursion instead of an outer loop. At each call, it finds the smallest item from the current index to the end, places that item at the current index, and then solves the rest of the list.

A list with zero or one item is already sorted, so the recursion stops immediately for those cases. The list is modified in place, and the same list is returned as required by the template.

```python
class Solution:
    def selectionSort(self, nums):
        def place_smallest(start):
            if start >= len(nums) - 1:
                return

            smallest = start
            for index in range(start + 1, len(nums)):
                if nums[index] < nums[smallest]:
                    smallest = index

            nums[start], nums[smallest] = nums[smallest], nums[start]
            place_smallest(start + 1)

        place_smallest(0)
        return nums
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursive call stack. The sorting itself uses no extra list.

Instead of placing the smallest value at the left, this code places the largest value at the right. The last unsorted position is fixed on every pass, so the search range becomes smaller each time.

Dry run for `[3, 1, 4, 2]`:

- The largest value in the full range is `4`, so it moves to index `3`.
- In `[3, 1, 2]`, the largest value is `3`, so it moves to index `2`.
- The remaining part becomes `[1, 2]` and is already in order after the next check.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] selectionSort(int[] nums) {
        for (int right = nums.length - 1; right > 0; right--) {
            int largest = right;

            for (int index = 0; index < right; index++) {
                if (nums[index] > nums[largest]) {
                    largest = index;
                }
            }

            int temp = nums[right];
            nums[right] = nums[largest];
            nums[largest] = temp;
        }

        return nums;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` comparisons in the worst case.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. The original array is changed.

This takes one minimum and one maximum during the same round. The minimum goes to the left boundary, while the maximum goes to the right boundary. Then both boundaries move inward.

For `[5, 2, 8, 1, 4]`, the first round puts `1` at the front and `8` at the back. The next round works only on the middle section. The checks also avoid swapping when the selected value is already at its target position.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> selectionSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            int smallest = left;
            int largest = left;

            for (int index = left + 1; index <= right; ++index) {
                if (nums[index] < nums[smallest]) {
                    smallest = index;
                }
                if (nums[index] > nums[largest]) {
                    largest = index;
                }
            }

            swap(nums[left], nums[smallest]);

            if (largest == left) {
                largest = smallest;
            }
            swap(nums[right], nums[largest]);

            ++left;
            --right;
        }

        return nums;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`. Each round still scans the current unsorted section.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. Arrays with one element or no elements need no work.

Selection sort normally swaps the chosen value into place. Here, the chosen minimum is moved by shifting the larger values one position to the right. That keeps equal values in their original order, so this version is stable.

The shift also handles an already sorted array without changing its values. An empty array is safe because the loop does not run.

```javascript
class Solution {
    selectionSort(nums) {
        for (let start = 0; start < nums.length - 1; start++) {
            let smallest = start;

            for (let index = start + 1; index < nums.length; index++) {
                if (nums[index] < nums[smallest]) {
                    smallest = index;
                }
            }

            const value = nums[smallest];
            for (let index = smallest; index > start; index--) {
                nums[index] = nums[index - 1];
            }
            nums[start] = value;
        }

        return nums;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`, including the search and the shifts.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. The input array is updated in place.

